  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Return of thunderstorms next week.\n\nEnjoy the clear skies while it lasts as rain and thunderstorms are \nlikely to return as early as Saturday night. A mid-level low will \npass through the northern Great Plains and swoop southeastward and \nhead towards the Appalachians. The big picture is that this system \nwill likely provide strong southwesterly winds and advect warm and \nmoist air into the Mid-Atlantic and Southeast. (Dew points may \nreturn into the 60s and 70s by the weekend). Model guidance \nexhibit a robust transport of vorticity through the region for \nthe first half of next week while this low persists. \nUnfortunately, models then start to disagree on how this low \nevolves. A possible scenario is the low dips so southward that \nit decouples itself from the mean flow and remains stagnant over\nthe eastern half of the United States. Another suggested \nscenario is that part of the low cuts off but has some of its \nenergy pulled away northeast by a stronger trough in Canada. The\nmain concern of this forecast is whether the low decides to \ndetach itself or not. If it does, confidence of several rounds \nof showers and thunderstorms for our area will increase. While \nthe severe threat looks to be on the lower side due to lapse \nrates of 5.0-5.5 C/km, there is a flood concern for next week. \nThe Weather Prediction Center has already issued a Marginal Risk\nfor parts of the area on Sunday and Monday, but it is currently\ntoo early to know how much rain we may get and what the \npotential impacts may be. Lastly, because models tend to \nstruggle with cutoff lows and the already present discrepancies,\nthe forecast for the latter half of next week is unclear.",
